  "g9-physics-c1-l1": {
    "id": "g9-physics-c1-l1",
    "chapterId": "g9-physics-c1",
    "title": "Distance, Displacement, Speed & Velocity",
    "desc": "Scalar vs Vector quantities, Distance vs Displacement (Displacement can be zero!), Average Speed vs Average Velocity, Uniform vs Non-Uniform Motion.",
    "content": "# Distance, Displacement, Speed & Velocity\n\n## 1. Learning Objectives\n- Understand rigorous Class 9 Physics principles of Distance vs Displacement & Velocity.\n- Apply kinematic derivations, Newton's laws, gravitation, and energy equations.\n- Solve numerical problems involving SI units, graphs, and momentum conservation.\n- Master examination derivations and conceptual reasoning for CBSE tests.\n\n## 2. Introduction\nIn Class 9 Physics, **Distance vs Displacement & Velocity** examines fundamental physical mechanics, gravitational field dynamics, fluid floatation, mechanical energy conservation, and wave acoustics. Scalar vs Vector quantities, Distance vs Displacement (Displacement can be zero!), Average Speed vs Average Velocity, Uniform vs Non-Uniform Motion.\n\n## 3. Detailed Concept Explanation\nThe study of **Distance vs Displacement & Velocity** provides students with quantitative problem-solving tools to derive motion equations, evaluate gravitational acceleration ($g = 9.8\\text{ m/s}^2$), verify mechanical energy conservation, and compute SONAR depth distances.\n\n### Key Physical Concepts\n1. **Kinematics & Force Derivations:** Graphically deriving $v = u+at, s = ut+\\frac{1}{2}at^2, v^2 = u^2+2as$, proving $F = ma$, and demonstrating momentum conservation ($m_1 u_1 + m_2 u_2 = m_1 v_1 + m_2 v_2$).\n2. **Gravitation & Fluid Floatation:** Evaluating $F = G \\frac{Mm}{d^2}$, $g = \\frac{GM}{R^2}$, $W = mg$, Archimedes' upthrust principle, and relative density.\n3. **Work-Energy & Wave Acoustics:** Proving mechanical energy conservation ($E_k + E_p = \\text{Const}$), commercial power units ($1\\text{ kWh} = 3.6 \\times 10^6\\text{ J}$), and $v = f \\lambda$.\n\nMastering **Distance vs Displacement & Velocity** requires understanding physical derivations, interpreting kinematic graphs, and executing multi-step numerical calculations using standard SI units.\n\n### Systematic Physics Inquiry Approach\n1. Identify given physical quantities and convert all metrics to standard SI units ($m, s, kg, N, J, W, Hz$).\n2. Select the appropriate kinematic, gravitational, or energy equation.\n3. Substitute numerical values and perform step-by-step algebraic simplification.\n4. Verify physical dimensional correctness and state the final result with units.\n\n## 4. Important Definitions\n- **Inertia:** The inherent property of a body by virtue of which it resists any change in its state of rest or uniform motion in a straight line.\n- **Universal Law of Gravitation:** Every particle of matter in the universe attracts every other particle with a force directly proportional to the product of their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them.\n- **Archimedes' Principle:** When a body is immersed fully or partially in a fluid, it experiences an upward buoyant force equal to the weight of the fluid displaced by it.\n\n## 5. Key Concepts\n- Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity: $a = \\frac{v-u}{t}$.\n- The slope of a Velocity-Time graph gives acceleration; the area under a Velocity-Time graph gives displacement ($s$).\n- Weight of an object on the Moon is $\\frac{1}{6}$th of its weight on Earth because $g_{\\text{moon}} = \\frac{1}{6} g_{\\text{earth}}$.\n\n## 6. Examples\n### Example 1: Derivation of $F = ma$\n*Solution:* Initial momentum $p_1 = mu$, Final momentum $p_2 = mv$. Change in momentum $\\Delta p = m(v-u)$. Rate of change of momentum $= \\frac{m(v-u)}{t} = ma$. By Second Law, $F \\propto ma \\implies F = kma$. In SI units where $k=1$, $F = ma$.\n\n### Example 2: Kinetic Energy Derivation\n*Solution:* Work done $W = F \\cdot s$. Since $F = ma$ and $v^2 - u^2 = 2as \\implies s = \\frac{v^2-u^2}{2a}$. Substituting:\n$$W = ma \\times \\frac{v^2-u^2}{2a} = \\frac{1}{2}m(v^2-u^2)$$\nIf initial velocity $u = 0$, then $W = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2 = E_k$.\n\n### Example 3: SONAR Distance Calculation\n*Solution:* A SONAR signal sent from a ship returns from the seabed in $3\\text{ s}$. If speed of sound in seawater is $1500\\text{ m/s}$, calculate depth. Distance travelled $2d = v \\times t = 1500 \\times 3 = 4500\\text{ m} \\implies d = 2250\\text{ m} = 2.25\\text{ km}$.\n\n## 7. Step-by-Step Explanation\n### Computational & Derivation Method\n1. State given physical variables with SI units ($u, v, a, t, s, m, F, g, E$).\n2. Write down the relevant physics derivation or formula.\n3. Perform substitution and step-by-step mathematical reduction.\n4. State final answer with proper unit symbols.\n\n## 8. Formulas / Rules\n- **Equations of Motion:**\n  $$v = u + at, \\quad s = ut + \\frac{1}{2}at^2, \\quad v^2 = u^2 + 2as$$\n- **Gravitational Acceleration & Law:**\n  $$F = G \\frac{M m}{d^2}, \\quad g = \\frac{GM}{R^2} = 9.8\\text{ m/s}^2$$\n- **Work, Energy & Wave Speed:**\n  $$E_k = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2, \\quad E_p = mgh, \\quad P = \\frac{W}{t}, \\quad v = f \\lambda$$\n\n## 9. Worked Problems / Activities\n### Worked Problem 1\nA bullet of mass $20\\text{ g}$ ($0.02\\text{ kg}$) is horizontally fired with a velocity of $150\\text{ m/s}$ from a pistol of mass $2\\text{ kg}$. Calculate the recoil velocity of the pistol.\n*Solution:* Initial total momentum $= 0$. Final total momentum $= m_1 v_1 + m_2 v_2 = (0.02 \\times 150) + (2 \\times v_{\\text{recoil}}) = 3 + 2 v_{\\text{recoil}} = 0 \\implies v_{\\text{recoil}} = -1.5\\text{ m/s}$ (opposite to bullet direction).\n\n### Worked Problem 2\nAn object of mass $500\\text{ g}$ has a volume of $350\\text{ cm}^3$. Will it float or sink in water (density $= 1\\text{ g/cm}^3$)?\n*Solution:* Density of object $= \\frac{\\text{Mass}}{\\text{Volume}} = \\frac{500}{350} = 1.43\\text{ g/cm}^3$. Since $1.43 > 1.0$, the object is denser than water and will sink!\n\n## 10. Try Yourself\n1. Derive the formula $s = ut + \\frac{1}{2}at^2$ using a velocity-time graph.\n2. How much work is done by a force of $10\\text{ N}$ moving an object by $5\\text{ m}$ at an angle of $60^\\circ$ to the force direction ($ \\cos 60^\\circ = 0.5$)?\n\n## 11. Common Mistakes\n- Forgetting to convert grams to kilograms or kilometers per hour to meters per second ($1\\text{ km/h} = \\frac{5}{18}\\text{ m/s}$).\n- Confusing mass ($kg$, constant everywhere) with weight ($N$, varies with $g$).\n\n## 12. Quick Revision & Key Takeaways\n- $v = u+at$, $s = ut+\\frac{1}{2}at^2$, $v^2 = u^2+2as$.\n- $F = ma$; $p = mv$; Recoil velocity uses momentum conservation.\n- $F = G \\frac{Mm}{d^2}$; $g = 9.8\\text{ m/s}^2$.\n- Archimedes Upthrust = Weight of displaced fluid.\n- $E_k = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2$; $E_p = mgh$; $v = f \\lambda$."
  },
